  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1226 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nMedium-long range model data is currently in rather fair agreement \nregarding the synoptic evolution early next week. Zonal flow will \nreamplify into southwest flow and week ridging over the region as a \ndeepening low pressure system approaches the western seaboard. In \ngeneral, next week's weather across the Panhandles will be more or \nless uneventful. Mild temperatures in the 70s to low 80s will recur \neach afternoon, with warmest days expected Wed and Thu. A tighter \nsurface pressure gradient will keep winds typically breezy for \nPanhandle standards, out of the south-southwest at 15-25 mph gusting \naround 35 mph daily.\n\nPrecipitation potential previously centered on late Monday into \nTuesday is trending downwards, as models have come to realize that \ndespite improving mid-level moisture, upper level lift will be \nmodest at best. This still supports about 10-15% chances for stray \nshowers/weak storms, but confidence in it raining is waning. Later \nin the week towards the weekend, global guidance is focusing on \nthe aforementioned deepening low to eventually eject over the \nPlains. Ample disagreement exists on the exact track and timing \nof this system, but early indications support the return of better\nmoisture and rain chances to the Panhandles. \n\nHarrel",
